{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 7,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.644,
            0.049,
            0.846,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Operations with Integers"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.088,
            0.316,
            0.111
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.122,
            0.67,
            0.193
          ],
          "content": "1. Using the token interpretation, find the values of: (a)\t 3 × (– 2) (b)\t (– 5) × (– 2) (c)\t (– 4) × (– 1) (d)\t (– 7) × 3"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.201,
            0.753,
            0.272
          ],
          "content": "2.\tIf 123 × 456 = 56088, without calculating, find the value of: (a)\t (– 123) × 456 (b)\t (– 123) × (– 456) (c)\t (123) × (– 456)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.282,
            0.688,
            0.304
          ],
          "content": "3. Try to frame a simple rule to multiply two integers."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.817,
            0.263,
            0.904,
            0.314
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.318,
            0.766,
            0.339
          ],
          "content": "Consider the numbers represented by the following tokens:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.197,
            0.374,
            0.228,
            0.392
          ],
          "content": "(a)"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.284,
            0.364,
            0.364,
            0.396
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.197,
            0.442,
            0.228,
            0.46
          ],
          "content": "(b)"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.285,
            0.415,
            0.439,
            0.481
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.197,
            0.53,
            0.226,
            0.548
          ],
          "content": "(c)"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.285,
            0.502,
            0.511,
            0.569
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.59,
            0.847,
            0.648
          ],
          "content": "We can see that all of them represent the number (– 2). Now, take 4 times each of these token sets. That is, place each set into the empty bag 4 times."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.657,
            0.802,
            0.718
          ],
          "content": "What integer do we get as the final answer in each case? Do we get different answers because the sets look different, or the same answer because they all represent – 2?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.816,
            0.655,
            0.904,
            0.724
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_7_image_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.731,
            0.81,
            0.754
          ],
          "content": "？ Check this for $5 \\times 4 ,$ by taking different token sets corresponding to 4."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.757,
            0.847,
            0.796
          ],
          "content": "We have seen that – 4 × 2 is the number obtained by removing 2 positive tokens from the empty bag 4 times."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.796,
            0.846,
            0.833
          ],
          "content": "We know that removing or subtracting a number is the same as adding its inverse. 1"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.834,
            0.803,
            0.872
          ],
          "content": "Using this, can – 4 × 2 be defined through a process of addition of tokens instead of removal of tokens?"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.818,
            0.822,
            0.904,
            0.874
          ],
          "content": "",
